      content:
          '- **PE History**: Massive bilateral PE 6 months ago. Presented with acute dyspnea, pleuritic chest pain, syncope. CT angiography showed bilateral main pulmonary artery emboli with saddle component. RV/LV ratio 1.4 (RV strain). Troponin elevated. BNP 890 pg/mL. Hemodynamically unstable (SBP 78 mmHg, HR 130). Treated with catheter-directed tPA in the IR suite. ICU stay 4 days, total hospitalization 8 days.\n- **Thrombophilia Workup**: Heterozygous Factor V Leiden. No other thrombophilia identified. Antiphospholipid antibodies negative. No prior VTE history. Was taking combined oral contraceptive pills (OCPs) — now discontinued.\n- **Current Anticoagulation**: Rivaroxaban 20 mg daily. Hematology recommends lifelong anticoagulation given unprovoked massive PE + Factor V Leiden.\n- **Persistent Symptoms**: Dyspnea with moderate exertion (walking uphill, stairs, carrying groceries). Exercise tolerance estimated at 50% of pre-PE baseline. Intermittent sharp right-sided chest pain with deep breathing. Fatigue requiring afternoon rest. Decreased stamina throughout the day.\n- **Functional Impact**: Unable to run. Avoids all vigorous activity. Uses elevator exclusively. Cannot sustain 8-hour workdays. Lost 2 major clients. Considering part-time practice.\n- **Psychological**: PTSD symptoms — flashbacks to the syncopal episode, avoidance of activities associated with elevated heart rate, hypervigilance to any chest sensation, catastrophic thinking ("every twinge means another PE"). PHQ-9 score 14 (moderate depression). GAD-7 score 16 (severe anxiety). Insomnia — wakes multiple times fearing she will die in her sleep.\n- **PMH**: Otherwise healthy. No prior cardiac or pulmonary disease.\n- **Medications**: Rivaroxaban 20 mg daily, escitalopram 10 mg daily (started 4 months ago, only partial response), melatonin 5 mg QHS, ibuprofen PRN for chest pain\n- **Social**: Married, 5-year-old daughter. Partner is supportive but frustrated by activity avoidance. Previous half-marathon runner (1:52 PR). Law firm partner (litigation).\n- **Review of Systems**: Dyspnea on exertion, fatigue, intermittent pleuritic chest pain, insomnia, anxiety, palpitations (especially with exertion or anxiety)',
