          '**Vitals**: BP 118/74 mmHg, HR 82 bpm, RR 16, SpO2 98% on RA at rest, Temp 36.8C, BMI 24.2.\n\n**Cardiovascular**: Regular rate and rhythm. Normal S1/S2. No murmurs. No RV heave. JVP not elevated. No peripheral edema. No calf tenderness or asymmetry.\n\n**Pulmonary**: Clear to auscultation bilaterally. No wheezing, crackles, or pleural rub. Symmetric chest expansion.\n\n**Musculoskeletal**: No joint abnormalities. Full ROM throughout. Mild generalized deconditioning. No muscle atrophy.\n\n**Neurologic**: Intact. No focal deficits.\n\n**Functional Testing**:\n- **6-Minute Walk Test**: 410 meters (predicted >550 meters for age — moderately reduced). SpO2 remained >95% throughout. HR: resting 82, peak 138, recovery at 2 min 105. Stopped at 6 minutes due to dyspnea and anxiety (Borg dyspnea 6/10). No desaturation.\n- **30-second chair stand**: 12 repetitions (low-normal)\n- **Grip strength**: R 26 kg, L 24 kg (normal for age)\n\n**Recent Investigations**:\n- **Echocardiogram** (5 months post-PE): LVEF 60%. RV mildly dilated. RVSP estimated 42 mmHg (mildly elevated — normal <35). Mild tricuspid regurgitation.\n- **CT Pulmonary Angiography** (4 months post-PE): Near-complete resolution of bilateral PE. Residual small filling defects in right lower lobe segmental arteries. No evidence of chronic organized thrombus.\n- **BNP**: 48 pg/mL (normal, down from 890 at presentation)\n- **CBC, CMP, TSH**: Normal. Hemoglobin 13.1 g/dL.',
