    Section(
      title: '+ Differentiate post-PE syndrome from CTEPH and deconditioning. What further workup is indicated?',
      content:
          '**Post-PE Syndrome**:\nA recognized clinical entity affecting **30-50% of patients after acute PE**. Characterized by persistent dyspnea, exercise intolerance, and reduced quality of life despite adequate anticoagulation and resolution of acute clot burden. Pathophysiology includes:\n- Residual pulmonary vascular obstruction (even small residual clot)\n- Pulmonary vascular remodeling and endothelial dysfunction\n- RV dysfunction/remodeling (even if LVEF is normal)\n- Deconditioning from prolonged activity avoidance\n- Psychological factors (PTSD, fear-avoidance, anxiety)\n\n**CTEPH — Must Be Ruled Out**:\nChronic thromboembolic pulmonary hypertension develops in **2-4% of PE survivors**. Key features:\n- Mean pulmonary artery pressure >=20 mmHg on right heart catheterization after >=3 months of anticoagulation\n- Organized, fibrotic thrombus causing mechanical obstruction (vs acute clot)\n- Progressive exertional dyspnea that worsens over time\n- Signs of RV failure (elevated JVP, edema, RV heave)\n- **Treatment**: Pulmonary thromboendarterectomy (PTE) is potentially curative — a surgical emergency to diagnose\n\n**This Patient\'s Risk for CTEPH**:\n- RVSP 42 mmHg (mildly elevated) at 5 months — concerning\n- Residual filling defects on CTA — could represent organized thrombus or resolving acute clot\n- Large initial clot burden is a CTEPH risk factor\n\n**Recommended Workup**:\n\n1. **V/Q Scan**: **Most sensitive screening test for CTEPH** — more sensitive than CTA for detecting chronic thromboembolic disease. A normal V/Q scan essentially excludes CTEPH. Mismatched perfusion defects suggest CTEPH.\n\n2. **Cardiopulmonary Exercise Testing (CPET)**: Gold standard for objective exercise limitation assessment. Will differentiate:\n- **Cardiac limitation**: Reduced VO2 peak, reduced O2 pulse, ventilatory equivalents suggest pulmonary vascular disease\n- **Deconditioning**: Low VO2 peak with normal cardiovascular parameters, early lactate threshold, high submaximal HR\n- **Ventilatory limitation**: Reduced breathing reserve, expiratory flow limitation\n- **Psychological/symptom amplification**: Stopping well before physiologic limits, hyperventilation pattern, normal physiologic parameters at peak exercise\n\n3. **Right Heart Catheterization**: If V/Q scan is abnormal or RVSP remains elevated, proceed to RHC for definitive hemodynamic assessment. This is the diagnostic gold standard for pulmonary hypertension.\n\n4. **Repeat Echocardiogram**: Reassess RV size and function, RVSP trend. If RVSP is normalizing, less concern for CTEPH.\n\n**Most Likely Diagnosis in This Patient**: Post-PE syndrome with significant deconditioning and psychologically-driven exercise avoidance (fear-avoidance behavior). However, CTEPH must be formally excluded given the mildly elevated RVSP.',
    ),
    Section(
      title: '+ CPET results show VO2 peak of 18 mL/kg/min (65% predicted), low anaerobic threshold, normal ventilatory equivalents, and hyperventilation at peak exercise. V/Q scan shows matched defects only. Interpret these findings.',
      content:
          '**CPET Interpretation**:\n\n- **VO2 peak 18 mL/kg/min (65% predicted)**: Moderately reduced. For a prior half-marathon runner, expected VO2 peak would be 38-42 mL/kg/min. Current level is consistent with a sedentary, mildly impaired individual.\n- **Low anaerobic threshold**: Early transition to anaerobic metabolism, consistent with deconditioning. Peripheral muscles are detrained and reach lactate threshold at lower workloads.\n- **Normal ventilatory equivalents (VE/VCO2)**: Argues AGAINST significant pulmonary vascular disease. In CTEPH or pulmonary hypertension, VE/VCO2 slope is typically >36 (elevated dead space ventilation). Normal value here is reassuring.\n- **Hyperventilation at peak exercise**: Breathing frequency disproportionately high relative to metabolic demand. Pattern consistent with **anxiety-driven hyperventilation** rather than physiologic limitation.\n- **V/Q scan — matched defects only**: **Effectively rules out CTEPH**. CTEPH produces mismatched perfusion defects (perfusion worse than ventilation). Matched defects are nonspecific.\n\n**Integrated Diagnosis**:\nThis patient has **post-PE syndrome** with two dominant components:\n1. **Cardiovascular deconditioning**: 6 months of activity avoidance has caused VO2 peak to drop from likely 40+ to 18 mL/kg/min. This is the primary driver of her functional limitation.\n2. **Anxiety-mediated exercise avoidance (fear-avoidance cycle)**: PTSD symptoms, catastrophic thinking, and hyperventilation pattern suggest a significant psychological barrier to exercise. Her perceived limitation exceeds her physiologic limitation.\n\n**CTEPH is excluded** based on normal VE/VCO2 and matched V/Q scan. The mildly elevated RVSP (42 mmHg) likely represents residual RV remodeling from the massive PE that is expected to improve with exercise and time.\n\n**Prognostic Significance**: Both deconditioning and anxiety-driven avoidance are **highly treatable** with graded exercise rehabilitation and cognitive behavioral therapy. This is an excellent candidate for pulmonary rehabilitation.',
