    Section(
      title: '+ Outline a graded exercise rehabilitation program for this patient, including anticoagulation considerations and psychological support.',
      content:
          '**Graded Exercise Rehabilitation Program for Post-PE Syndrome**:\n\n**Phase 1 — Supervised Introduction (Weeks 1-4)**:\nGoal: Break the fear-avoidance cycle. Demonstrate that exercise is safe.\n\n*Aerobic Training*:\n- **Setting**: Supervised cardiac/pulmonary rehab with ECG and SpO2 monitoring (reassurance function as much as medical necessity)\n- **Modality**: Walking on treadmill or cycling (cycling may feel safer initially — lower impact, seated position)\n- **Intensity**: Start BELOW anaerobic threshold. RPE 10-12 (Borg scale) = "light" to "fairly light." HR target: 60-70% of peak HR from CPET (~95-110 bpm).\n- **Duration**: 15-20 minutes continuous, with permission to stop if needed (patient controls the pace).\n- **Frequency**: 3 sessions/week supervised + 2 home walking sessions (20 minutes at comfortable pace)\n- **Key intervention**: After each session, explicitly debrief — "You just exercised for 20 minutes. Your oxygen level stayed normal. Your heart rhythm was perfect. Nothing bad happened. Let us notice that together."\n\n*Resistance Training*:\n- Begin bodyweight exercises: squats, lunges, wall push-ups, seated rows with band. 2 sets of 12-15 reps.\n- Focus on lower extremity and core to rebuild running foundation.\n\n**Phase 2 — Progressive Loading (Weeks 5-12)**:\n- **Aerobic**: Progress to 30-45 minutes at RPE 12-14 ("somewhat hard"). Introduce interval training: 3-minute moderate / 1-minute vigorous alternating. Add incline walking.\n- **Reintroduce running**: Week 6-8, begin walk/jog intervals on treadmill (1 min jog / 2 min walk x 10 cycles). Progress jog duration as tolerated.\n- **Resistance**: Progress to moderate-weight dumbbell or machine exercises. 3 sets of 10-12 reps.\n- **Monitoring**: Transition from continuous ECG to periodic SpO2 spot-checks as confidence builds.\n\n**Phase 3 — Return to Running (Weeks 12-24)**:\n- Follow structured return-to-running program (C25K-style progression).\n- Outdoor running introduction with GPS watch for pacing.\n- Progressive long runs: build by 10% weekly, with cutback weeks.\n- **Half-marathon timeline**: If progressing well, realistic to target a half-marathon at 9-12 months post-PE onset (6 months from now).\n\n**Anticoagulation Management During Exercise**:\n1. **Rivaroxaban timing**: Take with the largest meal of the day for absorption. Exercise timing does not need to change.\n2. **Contact/collision sports**: Avoid sports with significant bleeding risk (boxing, football, hockey). Running, cycling, swimming are safe.\n3. **Fall risk activities**: Caution with trail running (fall risk → bruising/bleeding). Road running and treadmill preferred initially.\n4. **NSAID interaction**: Discontinue ibuprofen — increased bleeding risk with rivaroxaban. Switch to acetaminophen for chest wall pain.\n5. **Medical alert**: Wear medical alert identification listing anticoagulant use.\n6. **Menstrual considerations**: Monitor for heavy menstrual bleeding on rivaroxaban (common in premenopausal women). Discuss with hematology and gynecology.\n\n**Psychological Support — Critical Component**:\n1. **Cognitive Behavioral Therapy (CBT)** for health anxiety and PTSD:\n- **Cognitive restructuring**: Challenge catastrophic thoughts ("chest tightness = another PE" reframed as "chest tightness can be normal with exercise after deconditioning")\n- **Graded exposure**: Systematic desensitization to feared stimuli (elevated HR, breathlessness, chest sensations) through progressive exercise\n- **Interoceptive exposure**: Intentionally induce elevated HR and breathlessness in a controlled setting to reduce fear response\n2. **SSRI optimization**: Escitalopram 10 mg has only partially helped. Consider increasing to 20 mg or switching to sertraline. Add short-acting anxiolytic (hydroxyzine 25 mg PRN) for acute anxiety episodes during exercise.\n3. **Trauma processing**: EMDR or prolonged exposure therapy for PTSD symptoms related to the syncopal/near-death experience.\n4. **Partner involvement**: Include partner in 1-2 sessions to address relationship strain from activity avoidance and discuss how to supportively encourage activity without pressuring.',
