    Section(
      title: '+ Discuss return-to-work planning for this patient and insurance/access considerations for post-PE rehabilitation.',
      content:
          '**Return-to-Work Planning**:\n\n1. **Current Limitation**: Cannot sustain 8-hour workdays due to fatigue and exercise intolerance. Has lost clients due to inability to perform full-day depositions.\n2. **Modified Work Plan**:\n- **Weeks 1-4**: Continue reduced schedule (part-time, 4-5 hours/day). Request accommodations: scheduled rest breaks, ability to sit during depositions, reduced travel.\n- **Weeks 5-12**: Progressive increase to 6-hour days. Alternate heavy deposition days with lighter administrative days.\n- **Weeks 12+**: Target full-time return as exercise tolerance improves.\n3. **ADA Considerations**: Post-PE syndrome with documented functional limitations may qualify as a disability under the ADA. Reasonable accommodations include modified schedule, rest breaks, and ergonomic workspace (standing desk to alternate positions).\n4. **Documentation**: Provide detailed functional capacity documentation to employer — frame in terms of specific limitations and expected recovery timeline rather than diagnosis alone.\n5. **Litigation stamina strategy**: For full-day depositions, advise frequent brief standing breaks (every 30-45 minutes), adequate hydration, and avoidance of heavy meals that provoke post-prandial fatigue.\n\n**Insurance and Access for Post-PE Rehabilitation**:\n\n1. **Pulmonary rehabilitation coverage**: Traditional pulmonary rehab is CMS-covered for COPD, ILD, and pulmonary hypertension — but **post-PE syndrome is NOT a standard-approved diagnosis for pulmonary rehab under Medicare**. Coverage varies by commercial payer.\n2. **Cardiac rehabilitation coverage**: If the patient has documented RV dysfunction (which she does — mildly dilated RV, elevated RVSP), cardiac rehab may be authorized under heart failure/cardiomyopathy diagnoses. Some programs classify massive PE with RV strain as qualifying.\n3. **Alternative coverage strategies**:\n- Code as physical therapy for deconditioning/functional impairment (ICD-10: R53.81, R26.89, Z87.821)\n- Psychology visits coded under PTSD/anxiety diagnoses (F43.10, F41.1)\n- Frame each discipline under its specific benefit category rather than bundled as "post-PE rehab"\n4. **Advocacy**: Post-PE rehabilitation is an emerging field. The PERT (Pulmonary Embolism Response Team) Consortium and PE research networks are advocating for formal recognition of post-PE syndrome as a rehabilitative diagnosis. Document outcomes to contribute to the evidence base.\n\n**Hematology Coordination**:\n- Lifelong anticoagulation monitoring (annual reassessment of bleeding risk vs recurrence risk)\n- Genetic counseling for Factor V Leiden (autosomal dominant — 50% chance of inheritance by daughter)\n- Contraception counseling: OCPs are contraindicated. Progestin-only methods or non-hormonal options recommended.\n- Pre-pregnancy planning if desired: requires transition from rivaroxaban to LMWH (rivaroxaban is teratogenic)',
