    Section(
      title: '+ How would you address this patient\'s exercise-related fear and PTSD response to help her take the first step?',
      content:
          '**Model Response**:\n\n**1. Stop. Do not push. Step away from the treadmill.**\n\'Let us step away from the machine for a moment. There is no pressure to get on it right now. Let us sit down and talk.\'\n[Move to a chair. The physical act of stepping away from the trigger reduces the acute anxiety response and shows her she has control.]\n\n**2. Validate the fear as rational, not irrational**:\n\'What you are feeling is not weakness and it is not irrational. Six months ago, your heart was racing and you collapsed and nearly died. Your brain learned from that experience that a fast heart rate means danger. That is your survival instinct doing exactly what it is supposed to do. The problem is that it cannot tell the difference between a dangerous fast heart rate and a healthy one from exercise.\'\n\n**3. Provide the medical reassurance she needs to hear**:\n\'I want to share some medical facts with you. First, your CTEPH workup is negative — you do not have chronic clot disease. Second, you are on rivaroxaban, which is actively preventing new clots right now. Third, exercise actually reduces your risk of another PE — it improves blood flow and reduces stasis, which is one of the main causes of clots. Sitting still out of fear is physiologically more dangerous than moving.\'\n\n**4. Normalize the PTSD response**:\n\'What you are experiencing has a name. It is called a fear-avoidance response, and it is very common after life-threatening medical events. About one-third of PE survivors develop significant anxiety or PTSD symptoms. You are not alone in this, and it is treatable.\'\n\n**5. Offer an achievable first step — not the treadmill**:\n\'Here is what I would like to try instead of the treadmill today. Can you walk with me to the end of this hallway and back? Just a walk. At your pace. I will be right beside you, and we will watch your oxygen level on this monitor the entire time. You are in complete control — you can stop any time. Can we try that?\'\n[Start with the least threatening activity. Success breeds confidence.]\n\n**6. Use interoceptive labeling in real time**:\n[During the walk]: \'Notice your heart rate is 95 right now. How does your body feel? What thoughts are coming up? Your oxygen is 98 percent — completely normal. Your heart is doing exactly what it should be doing. Let us keep walking and just notice the sensations without judging them.\'\n\n**7. Celebrate the success and plan next steps**:\n\'You just walked 200 feet. Your heart rate went up and came back down. Your oxygen stayed perfect. Nothing bad happened. I know that might not feel like much compared to half-marathons, but this was the hardest step — the first one. Can we agree to come back on Wednesday and try again? Maybe a little further?\'\n\n**8. Address the core fear — being there for her daughter**:\n\'You said your daughter needs her mother. She does. And the best thing you can do for her is to rebuild your health so you can be active and present for her. Right now, fear is taking you away from her just as much as illness would. Let us fight for that together.\'\n\n**Key principles demonstrated**: Removing the trigger, validating the PTSD response as adaptive, providing medical evidence for safety, normalizing the experience, offering graded exposure starting below the fear threshold, real-time interoceptive processing, celebrating success, connecting to core values (daughter), collaborative therapeutic alliance.',
